

● Moved the audit log up to the application layer — 3142bfa.

  item_registered     owner_id · group_id · item_id · item_tag
  item_unregistered   owner_id · group_id · item_id · item_tag + orphan_children

  This was a placement problem. With the audit sitting in the domain service, it structurally couldn't carry
  the actor — audit exists to answer "who did this," but the domain must not know about sessions. I just followed the path the earlier branch already cut.

  Two things came along with it:

  - Unlinking reads before it deletes — once membership is dropped, the tag meant for the audit log can
  no longer be found in that group. Missing or someone else's group, the domain service rejects both with the same code, so the judgment doesn't fork.
  - Two stale statements — item_service's docstring saying "records that fact to the audit log," and a
  comment explaining the read as "to get the tag that goes into the audit log." Now that audit has moved out,
  the only reason left for that read is the 404 check.

  One of the 3 safety-net checks measures whether the audit is a single line — if it also lingers in the
  service it becomes two lines, the easiest mistake to make in a migration like this.

  Full suite: 1791 passed / 1 failed (someone else's worker's territory)
  Mutation: dropping owner_id from the shared payload catches 3

  What's left is the second finding from earlier — list_items sends back a raw list[Item], register_item
  a raw Item, straight as the response. That's the spot grid §2 flags as "Item/Child can't rename fields
  right now" — the earlier branch closed it today with DetailResult, but this one is still open.

  Next up is that ItemResult — want me to keep going?

